        <div id="first-month" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>A Sensible First Month</h2>
<p>
            The equipment decision is the easy part. What determines whether it was worth the money
            is what happens in weeks two through six, and a deliberately conservative start is the
            single best predictor of a habit that survives.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Week one:</strong> fifteen minutes at an easy walking pace, three times. The
            only goal is getting on and off confidently and finding a speed you could hold while
            talking. <strong>Week two:</strong> twenty minutes, three or four times, same speed. <strong>Week three:</strong> twenty-five to thirty minutes, and either raise the speed
            slightly or add 2% incline — not both. <strong>Week four:</strong> thirty to
            thirty-five minutes, four times.
          </p>
          <p>
            That arrives at roughly two hours of activity a week without a single hard session, and
            it is far more valuable than three punishing weeks followed by nothing. Add minutes
            before speed, and incline before speed: incline raises effort without raising impact or
            the risk of catching a foot, which makes it the better lever early on. Our <a href="/treadmill-workouts/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">guide to treadmill workouts</a> covers what comes after that first month, and the <a href="/12-3-30-treadmill-workout/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">12-3-30 guide</a> includes a gentler build-up if structured incline walking appeals later.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="placement" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Where You Put It Decides Whether You Use It</h2>
<p>
            This is the most under-rated decision in the whole purchase. A treadmill in a cold
            garage or an unheated spare room gets used in April and not in January. A treadmill in
            a room you already spend time in, with a plug nearby and something to watch, gets used.
          </p>
          <p>
            Leave clear space behind the deck — it is where you go if you lose your footing, and it
            matters most while you are still getting used to the machine. Avoid thick carpet, which
            draws fibres into the motor, blocks its ventilation and lets the frame shift under load;
            our <a href="/treadmill-on-carpet/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">carpet guide</a> covers the fix if you have no alternative. And check the ceiling if you are tall,
            because incline raises the deck several inches at the point where you stand.
          </p>
          <p>
            The full clearance and delivery arithmetic is in our <a href="/treadmill-dimensions-space-requirements/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">dimensions guide</a>.
            Do it before ordering rather than after, because the most common delivery failure is a
            machine that fits the room and not the stairwell.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="maintenance" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>The Maintenance Nobody Tells Beginners About</h2>
<p>
            First-time owners are rarely told that a treadmill needs anything, and the result is
            the most common cause of early failure: belt-to-deck friction rising until it takes the
            motor and controller board with it. The total maintenance a home treadmill needs is
            under an hour a year.
          </p>
          <p>
            Three habits cover almost all of it. Wipe the belt and the deck edges after use, so
            dust and sweat do not build into an abrasive film. Vacuum around and under the machine
            monthly. And lubricate the belt roughly <a href="/how-often-to-lubricate-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>every 40 to 50 hours</a> of use — a ten-minute job
            most owners never do once, and the single highest-return thing you can do for the
            machine's lifespan.
          </p>
          <p>
            One warning before you buy any lubricant: check your manual first. Some decks are
            pre-waxed and are ruined by silicone, and household products are worse than doing
            nothing. Our <a href="/treadmill-belt-lubrication/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">lubrication guide</a> has the step-by-step and the <a href="/treadmill-maintenance/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">maintenance schedule</a> has the full year.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="verdict" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Final Recommendations</h2>
<p>
            For most first-time buyers the <strong>Sole F63</strong> is the machine. It has a
            hydraulic fold one person can manage, a console of physical buttons you can operate
            without reading anything, and carries no subscription — so its cost is its
            price. It is more machine than a beginner strictly needs, and that is why it survives
            the transition from beginner to regular user.
          </p>
          <p>
            On a firmer budget the <strong>NordicTrack T Series 5</strong> is a reasonable buy for
            walking, provided you accept the short deck and the 10 mph ceiling. If you want guided
            content to supply the structure, the <strong>ProForm Carbon TLX</strong> gives you that
            through your own tablet on iFIT's cheaper Train membership, and it still works without paying.
          </p>
          <p>
            Whichever arrives, spend the first month walking slowly and put the machine somewhere
            you already are. Those two decisions matter more than anything on the spec sheet.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Common Mistakes Buying a First Treadmill</h2>
          <p>These four account for most abandoned machines, and none of them is a specification problem.</p>
          <ol class="ed-mistakes not-prose">
            <li>
              <h3>Buying for the person you intend to become</h3>
              <p>A $2,500 machine chosen for a version of yourself who runs five days a week is very good at things you will not do for a year, and it often comes with guilt attached. Buy for the person you are in week three. If the habit sticks, upgrading in two years with real knowledge of what you want beats guessing now.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Doing too much in week one</h3>
              <p>Three hard sessions in the first week produce several days of soreness that break the habit before it forms. Fifteen minutes at a conversational pace is the right week-one session even when it feels absurdly easy. Add minutes before speed and incline before speed, and the routine will still be running in month six.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Putting it in the garage</h3>
              <p>A treadmill in an unheated garage or spare room gets used in April and not in January. Put it somewhere you already spend time, with a plug nearby and something to watch. Placement is the most under-rated decision in the whole purchase and it costs nothing to get right.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Never lubricating the belt</h3>
              <p>Belt-to-deck friction rising unchecked is the most common cause of early treadmill death, taking the motor and controller board with it. Lubrication is a ten-minute job every 40 to 50 hours that most first-time owners never do once. Check the manual before buying any product — some decks are pre-waxed and silicone ruins them.</p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </div>

        <!-- The bottom line -->
        <div id="bottom-line" class="ed-bottomline">
          <h2>The bottom line</h2>
          <p>
            Spend $700 to $1,200, buy the simplest console you can find, and put the machine in a room
            you already use. The <strong class="text-white">Sole F63</strong> is the first
            treadmill we recommend most often — easy to start, easy to store, and still the right
            machine when you are no longer a beginner.
          </p>
          <p>
            Working to a tighter budget? The <a href="/best-treadmill-under-1000-reviews/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">under-$1,000 guide</a> covers that tier properly. Need structure to keep going? Guided content is worth
            paying for — just <a href="/best-treadmills-without-subscriptions/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">count the five-year cost</a> before you commit.
          </p>
        </div>

