        <!-- FAQ -->
        <section id="faq" class="ed-faq scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
          <details open>
            <summary><h3>How much should a beginner spend on a treadmill?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Between $700 and $1,200 for almost everyone. Below $700 the compromises stop being cosmetic — motors under 2.5 CHP, decks under 55 inches and frames light enough to move underfoot. Above $1,200 you are buying for running rather than for starting, and none of it helps someone walking at 3 mph four times a week.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What treadmill specs can a beginner ignore?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Peak horsepower, which describes a burst the motor cannot sustain; top speed, since 12 mph is a five-minute mile and you will spend a year between 2.5 and 5 mph; screen size, which makes a treadmill larger rather than better; and decline, which is genuinely useful only for downhill race preparation.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>How long does it take to assemble a treadmill?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              It varies widely with the machine, and heavier ones genuinely require two people. It is worth more attention than most specifications, because a treadmill that is difficult to build often sits in its box for a fortnight — and that fortnight is when the initial motivation is spent.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>How often should a beginner use a treadmill?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Three to four times a week, starting at around fifteen minutes at a pace you could hold a conversation at. Build minutes before speed, and add incline before adding speed. Roughly two hours a week by the end of month one is a strong outcome, and far more valuable than three hard weeks followed by nothing.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Should a beginner buy a folding treadmill?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              If the machine has to share a room, yes — but judge the fold mechanism rather than the label. A hydraulic assist that one person can work easily genuinely gets used; a manual latch needing effort and two people means the machine stays up permanently, at which point its unfolded dimensions are the real ones.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Do I need to maintain a new treadmill?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Yes, and it is rarely mentioned at the point of sale. Wipe the belt and deck edges after use, vacuum around the machine monthly, and lubricate the belt every 40 to 50 hours of use. That is under an hour a year and it roughly doubles the machine's life. Check your manual first — some decks are pre-waxed and are ruined by silicone.
            </p>
          </details>
        </section>

